    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The first kingdom to unite the Lao lands along the middle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mekong-river">Mekong</a>, founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fa-ngum">Fa Ngum</a> in 1353.</li><li>A Buddhist mandala state of semi-autonomous principalities, ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luang-prabang">Luang Prabang</a> and from 1560 from Vientiane.</li><li>A succession crisis after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sourigna-vongsa">Sourigna Vongsa</a>'s death split it into the kingdoms of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-luang-phrabang">Luang Phrabang</a> and Vientiane in 1707.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samsenthai">Samsenthai</a>'s name means 'three hundred thousand Tai', traditionally explained by a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> that counted 300,000 Tai men available for royal service.</p></aside>
    </section>

